/*
# Button

A labeled action control. The action is its `onClick` — write it at the use site
exactly as on any View; `Button` adds the interaction states, the themed look, and
keyboard activation (Space/Enter drives the same `onClick`). `primary = true` fills
with the accent; the default is the quiet control look.

```declare-fragment
Button [ label = "Save", primary = true ]
Button [ label = "Add", iconLeft = "PlusIcon" ]
Button [ label = "Options", menu = { app.optionsMenu } ]      // chevron implied
Button [ iconLeft = "CloseIcon" ]                             // icon only
```

Three slots in a row — `iconLeft`, the label, `iconRight` — laid out by
`SimpleLayout` and sized from `contentWidth`, so an absent label or icon costs
nothing and needs no special case: an invisible child is skipped and its space
reclaimed. A `menu` supplies the right icon on its own (`control.declare`).

The fill walks the four-rung ladder — `control` at rest, `controlHover`,
`controlPressed`, and `controlSelected` while its menu is open. Before the ladder
existed this read `down || hot ? controlActive : control`, which meant **hovering
a button looked exactly like pressing it**.

## label
The button's text. `""` (the default) leaves it iconless-and-wordless, which is
only useful with an `icon` — a button with neither has nothing to press.

## primary
Marks this as the affirmative action: it fills with the accent and takes
`theme.accentText` for its ink. One per group of choices — a dialog's Save, not
its Cancel. Purely a style decision; it changes nothing about behaviour.

```declare-fragment
Button [ label = "Cancel" ],
Button [ label = "Save", primary = true ]
```

## gap
Space between the icon and the label, when both are present.

## padH
Horizontal padding inside the button, each side. The button sizes to its content
plus twice this, so widening it is how you make a short label less cramped.

```declare-fragment
Button [ label = "OK", padH = 28 ]                  // a short word, given room
Button [ label = "Add", iconLeft = "PlusIcon", gap = 12 ]
```

## inkColor
The one ink decision, so a button's icon and its label always agree: the accent
text colour when `primary`, the ordinary text colour otherwise. `textColor`
follows it. Override this rather than `textColor` alone, or the icon and the
words drift apart.

```declare-fragment
class DangerButton extends Button [ inkColor = { primary ? white : crimson } ]
```

## baseline
The baseline a row aligns this control by — its label's, in the control's own
coordinates. A `SimpleLayout` or `WrappingLayout` with `align = baseline` sits the
button's text on the same line as a neighbouring `Text` or `Markdown`; without this
declaration the layout would refuse the button by name (a baseline is claimed, never
guessed from a box). Declared here so every button carries it; an icon-only button still
answers its (invisible) label's line.

```declare-fragment
row: View [ layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 10, align = baseline ],
    Text [ fontSize = 18, text = "Ready?" ],
    Button [ label = "Save", primary = true ]      // its label sits on the text's line
    ]
```

## height
The theme decides, when it has an opinion: `theme.buttonHeight` sets it (a platform
theme's native control height); a theme that leaves it unset gets 40. A `height` at
the use site overrides both.

## cornerRadius
A theme that rounds buttons by a fixed radius sets `theme.buttonRadius`; it is capped
at half the height, so a short button goes fully pill without a squared corner.
Otherwise the shared `controlRadius` every control uses.

## width
Content-sized: the run — icon, label, icon — plus `padH` each side. Give it a `width`
and the run centres inside it, so a dialog's equal-width row keeps every label centred.

## opacity
Disabled dims the whole control, icon and label alike, rather than recolouring it.

## fill
The state ladder, one rung per interaction fact, first match wins: a primary button
is the accent whatever its state; an open menu holds the selected tint, so the
button stays lit while its menu is up; pressed beats hovered; at rest, the quiet
`control`. Every rung is a theme token, so a theme restyles every button's states
at once.

## stroke
A hairline only where the theme wants one: themes that border their secondary
buttons set `theme.buttonBorder`; flat themes leave it null and get no stroke. A
primary button is never bordered — the accent fill is its edge.

## press()
The activation path, called by a click and by Space/Enter. It flashes the press
tint, opens an attached `menu` if there is one, then calls `onClick`. Override
`onClick` for ordinary use; override this only to change what activation *means*.

## onClick
Your handler. Fires after the press is delivered, so the affordance has already
run — put the work here, not in `press()`.

## textColor
Follows `inkColor`, the button's one ink decision, so the label and the icon can never disagree. Override `inkColor`, not this.
*/

include [ "control.declare", "icons/icon.declare" ]

class Button extends Control [
    // the baseline a row aligns this control by: its label's (declared, never discovered)

    baseline: number = { this.run.y + this.run.cap.y + this.run.cap.baseline },
    height = { typeof theme.buttonHeight == "number" ? theme.buttonHeight : 40 },
    cornerRadius = { typeof theme.buttonRadius == "number" ? Math.min(theme.buttonRadius, height / 2) : theme.controlRadius },
    label:   string = "",
    primary: boolean = false,

    iconSize = 18,
    gap:  number = 8,
    padH: number = 16,

    onClick() { },
    press()   { flashPress(); if (menu != null) openMenu(); onClick() },

    // one ink decision, so a primary button's icon and its label agree

    inkColor: Color = { primary ? theme.accentText : theme.text },
    textColor = { inkColor },

    // The RUN sizes to its own content and centres itself in the button; the
    // button's default width is the run plus symmetric padding. Two objects, so
    // the two facts stay independent: a button given an explicit `width` (a
    // dialog's equal-width row, a toolbar's fixed slot) still centres what it
    // holds, which a packed layout on the button itself would not.
    //
    // Not flexing Spacers: SimpleLayout reads the view's own extent to divide
    // slack, and the extent here is derived FROM the content — that closes a
    // cycle. A self-sizing child breaks it, because the run's width never looks
    // outward.

    width   = { this.run.width + this.padH * 2 },
    opacity = { disabled ? 0.45 : 1 },

    fill = { primary   ? theme.accent
           : menuOpen  ? theme.controlSelected
           : down      ? theme.controlPressed
           : hot       ? theme.controlHover
           :             theme.control },

    // platforms whose secondary buttons are BORDERED say so in the theme
    // (Cupertino's hairline, Fluent's 1px) — flat systems leave it null

    stroke  = { !primary && theme.buttonBorder != null ? stroke(1, theme.buttonBorder) : null },

    run: View [ height = { parent.height },
        x = { (parent.width - this.width) / 2 },
        width = { this.contentWidth },
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = { classroot.gap } ],

        ico:   IconHost [ kind = { classroot.iconLeft },  ink = { classroot.inkColor } ],
        cap:   TextLabel [ wrap = false, fontWeight = semibold,
            visible = { classroot.label != "" }, text = { classroot.label } ],
        trail: IconHost [ kind = { classroot.rightIcon }, ink = { classroot.inkColor } ]
        ]
    ]
